WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, we heard from several people

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.680
whose job is not to use these tools, but to get

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.880
other engineers using them, which turns out to be a completely different problem

4
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.880
with a completely different set of ways to get it wrong.

5
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.560
Let's first hear from Ian Thomas at Meta, who took a community from heel

6
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.160
dragging and ad hoc usage to 40 times the size,

7
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.280
with weekly usage above 80%.

8
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:26.840
Yes. Hi.

9
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.880
I'm Ian. I am a software engineer based in the UK.

10
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.440
I work remotely from Yorkshire, which is a wonderful juxtaposition

11
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.360
to working on VR and

12
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.400
and metaverse products. Lots of people were working on operational work.

13
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.320
They had lots of on-call overhead, bugs coming in, front and center,

14
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.800
things that everyone has to deal with in their day to day work,

15
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.000
and it takes up a significant amount of your capacity.

16
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.720
We're also trying to build this culture of AI adoption,

17
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:56.600
and we knew that AI

18
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.880
tooling was going to be important,

19
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.600
and it was going to be the way that engineering evolved.

20
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.600
But what we were finding was there was a bit of heel-dragging

21
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.760
going on, and the adoption wasn't uniform.

22
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.600
It was, it was ad hoc at best.

23
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.400
People were finding low quality outcomes.

24
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.440
They were dragging.

25
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.240
They were trying to use the wrong tools for the wrong problem.

26
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.040
They didn't always have the same processes.

27
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.080
And where there was success, it was kind of kept in a fairly small

28
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.840
area of knowledge, siloed information within their teams,

29
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.120
and there wasn't a great deal of value being perceived.

30
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.640
People weren't seeing the return on their investment in time. So

31
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.000
we were seeing this was going to be quite a difficult problem for us to bridge.

32
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.080
And to fast forward you to the end.

33
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.640
What we did achieve in the end with this, with this work that I was part of,

34
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.120
we grew an organic community that was over 40 times bigger than when we started.

35
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.880
I think at the time when I stopped working on this,

36
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.840
we were well over 500 people and that was at the start

37
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.840
of this year in January,

38
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.960
well over 80% of people

39
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.280
were using these tools weekly, which was up from under half.

40
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.840
And I would say consistently now, I think I see this in the mid to high 90s.

41
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.520
So we're really high up in the adoption curve now.

42
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.720
And for certain workflows we're seeing significant time gains.

43
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.200
We built a maturity model that I'm going to share with you later today.

44
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.680
And there were patterns that we invested in.

45
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.360
And we found through experiments that we

46
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.160
then started to spread throughout the company.

47
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.600
And it became a pattern that was used widely,

48
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.320
not just in these small, siloed groups of success.

49
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.120
We also heard from Rob Willoughby and Simon of the Stanford

50
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.600
QA lab, who have been studying this across 150,000 engineers.

51
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.200
They're seeing the widest performance spread they've ever measured.

52
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.360
Simon set out to disprove the 10x engineer.

53
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.160
He now thinks he's watching one appear.

54
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.520
It's a significant difference.

55
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.680
It's the biggest difference that we've ever seen to be honest

56
00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.480
I started this study in 2020.

57
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:07.920
That's going to be, you know, thought about how do we measure productivity.

58
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.320
For me, I had worked in Silicon Valley, and the 10x engineer

59
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.520
was something that I really liked, to a certain extent,

60
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.480
and I wanted to show that it doesn't exist and it didn't exist.

61
00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.720
But over time, we're starting to see it now.

62
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.880
So people that know how to create agents, people that know how to work, they

63
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.760
they achieve significantly better outcomes.

64
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.240
Now. On top of that,

65
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.000
and I think this is a really interesting slide in terms of

66
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.480
how performance is shifting.

67
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:52.600
So once here is the top and the bottom again.

68
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.560
And we

69
00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.520
saw a pretty high rank ability direct.

70
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.280
The pivoted was 0.70.

71
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:02.840
Yeah.

72
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.160
And policy it dropped to 0.45.

73
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.040
So we believe also like that the tactics that anybody looking to get to perform

74
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.160
no longer enabling you to get top performer today.

75
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.280
So what we believe is what happens

76
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.240
to be honest, a lot of other people from the

77
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.480
the top performers, they moved to the top performance.

78
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.880
One hypothesis is that like these were people

79
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.080
that were coding would be using the teams.

80
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.280
And now in terms of how maybe some of that automated, some

81
00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.720
that have got 15 way, and now they became the top performers.

82
00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.200
So arguably that's the hypothesis is right.

83
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.040
I don't understand why, but we observed that it happens.

84
00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.120
They were busy helping others deliver something delivered good outcomes.

85
00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.640
But now they have time and they're killing it.

86
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.440
Daniel Jones and Tomasz Maj ran an AI training program across

87
00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.840
an entire enterprise.

88
00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.600
And their starting point is a warning.

89
00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:12.360
If you are not already good at shipping software, pointing

90
00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.360
agents at the problem doesn't help.

91
00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:15.720
It makes it worse.

92
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:17.280
The first thing that we did is a process called discovery.

93
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.440
thing that we did is a process called discovery.

94
00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:23.760
So for folks who aren't consultants, this is where you go into an organization

95
00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.760
and you figure out what's the current lay of the land.

96
00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.160
And this is a really important thing to do because of this very authentic

97
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.160
DORA report quote, might be.

98
00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.600
But in the 2025 DORA report, there were a couple.

99
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.560
There was one that was, I think, August-ish.

100
00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.960
It made the point, after looking at a couple of thousand companies

101
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.960
that if you are not doing software development well at the moment and you

102
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.800
throw agentic coding at the problem, things are going to get worse.

103
00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.240
You're optimizing a system, you're making one part

104
00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.560
of an interconnected system go really fast.

105
00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.560
You will expose bottlenecks elsewhere.

106
00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.720
If you're doing software delivery well and you start adopting agentic coding,

107
00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:09.320
then things will go much faster

108
00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.320
and you'll be on a hockey stick of like exponential performance.

109
00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.760
The tricky part is that nobody knows where this balance point is.

110
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.800
Like how crap do you have to be?

111
00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.360
Is it, how bad at software development do you have to be to go slower?

112
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.920
Like, are you in a good place or not?

113
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.680
And as an industry, we haven't quite figured that out, but the kind of things

114
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:35.400
that we needed to look at across the various different teams and cultures

115
00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.400
at Odevo were things like CI/CD. Do you have a platform?

116
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.320
This is a current bugbear of mine with

117
00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.480
I shouldn't tell too many tangents about other customers,

118
00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.440
but if you don't have a platform

119
00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.960
like you can't ship code reliably and quickly, and then you're just

120
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.320
going to generate all this code and have no where to go with it.

121
00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.520
Do you have tests?

122
00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.560
If agents can't run tests to find out they've broken your software,

123
00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.360
don't be surprised when they break your software.

124
00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.680
If you don't have coding standards.

125
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.600
If the humans in your organization don't agree what good looks like,

126
00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.720
then there's a very low chance that an agent is going to be able

127
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.320
to produce code that your team is going to approve of.

128
00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.560
So there are lots of things that are worth looking at.

129
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:19.800
And the optimist in me likes to think that maybe this is another opportunity

130
00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:23.160
as an industry, that we get to look at those fundamentals and go, look,

131
00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.840
we really need to improve these.

132
00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.200
And to close, Hannah Foxwell on the part that gets the least airtime.

133
00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:32.240
Careers that make sense for the skills we actually need.

134
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.560
Now, her three anchors are: build something worth building,

135
00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:40.040
Speed requires safety and people matter.

136
00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:41.960
People matter.

137
00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.080
Like, I want to make sure that every engineer has a sustainable on-call rota.

138
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.080
I want to make sure that we have a language to discuss, like

139
00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.120
our error budgets and our availability targets, and invest appropriately.

140
00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.520
And I want to do realistic career planning for the skills that we need

141
00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.560
today, not the skills that we needed yesterday.

142
00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.520
I am trashing heavy planning processes.

143
00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.960
Two-week sprints are absolutely too long right now, and mandatory code reviews

144
00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.560
feels like an unsustainable practice that's going to need

145
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.840
to be replaced with some other ways of assuring quality.

146
00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.840
I'm definitely shifting left on peer review using spec-driven development,

147
00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.960
and I'm going to encourage all of you in this room, and especially the managers,

148
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.800
to think about how we create people who are less T-shaped

149
00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:34.640
and more of a broken comb, people with diverse areas of depth,

150
00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.920
because those are the sort of people who are going to thrive in this new age.

151
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:41.240
We are

152
00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.400
all on a journey and we do not know where we're heading,

153
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.600
and I find it exciting, but it can also be daunting.

154
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.400
And so as you leave here, hopefully I've given you some ideas

155
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:57.200
to experiment with or to help you address some of the new challenges.

156
00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:00.280
I would just urge all of you

157
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.280
to experiment with empathy because it is not an easy thing

158
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:10.760
to change, to change your discipline so fundamentally and at such speed.

159
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.360
So yes, we do need to try new things.

160
00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.800
We need to share our learning, but do it with people.

161
00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.240
Don't do it to them.

162
00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.560
And so

163
00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.400
here are the things that I'm keeping.

164
00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.600
No matter what, no matter how good agents become,

165
00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.200
I'm always going to build an organization that focuses on the user.

166
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.320
Build something worth building.

167
00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.760
Solve a real problem.

168
00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.040
I'm going to be investing in ways that we can move safely with speed.

169
00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.680
And I'm going to always, always focus on the people

170
00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.320
because people matter and we need to create teams

171
00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:48.360
that are joyful to work in

172
00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.400
and careers that are worth pursuing.

173
00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.200
Thanks for watching.

174
00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.840
Be sure to join us for the next AI DevCon this November in New York City.

175
00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.800
Visit AI DevCon to learn more and book your ticket.
